e this code  and compile multiple versions of the  code that can handle  different types uh efficiently so uh  some of the  information we can specialize based on  is things like low level type  information  uh register allocation state and also  object types and global variable types  so how does this work well uh a very  simple example here  suppose we have a loop that does a lot  of  bitwise and so each iteration it will  compute the bitwise and of  v and i and also increment uh the loop  counter i so this is actually from  the sunspider bitwise and benchmark  so to a compiler this loop will look a  little bit more like this  that is each operator is actually  sort of like a function that has some  some complex logic hidden inside  so the less than function the less than  operator is a function  call and so is the ad and so is the  bitwise and  and inside an operator like bitwise and  typically what's going to be done is  that we're going to check the type of  of the inputs x and y and then we'll  have a fast path here that will use  an optimized machine instruction that  does bitwise and  only in integers and if x and y are not  integers we're going to have a slope at  where we  we sort of do the the general purpose  operation so we're going to convert  x and y to integers and then do uh a  general  a bitwise n on that  same thing goes for for the add  operation typically we'll we'll do a  type test and we'll have a fast path  and then if x and y are not actually  integers we're going to handle  the case for for strings and all the  other types elsewhere  there's an extra complication in that  add  will have to do an overflow check it'll  have to check that the results of the ad  actually fits in a 32-bit integer and if  it doesn't  it will have to uh convert x and y to  double values and do the operation on on  a double instead  and similarly for uh  for less than we're also going to have a  fast and slow path  and so putting it all together the loop  is going 